{"prompt": "A shirt costs $25 after a 20% discount was applied twice. What was the original price?", "response": "Let me work through this carefully. Two successive 20% discounts means the price was multiplied by 0.8 twice: original x 0.8 x 0.8 = original x 0.64. So original = 25 / 0.64 = 39.0625. Check: 39.0625 x 0.8 = 31.25, and 31.25 x 0.8 = 25. Correct. The original price was $39.06 (to the cent)."}
{"prompt": "If all Bloops are Razzies and no Razzies are Lazzies, can a Bloop be a Lazzie?", "response": "Step 1: every Bloop is a Razzie. Step 2: no Razzie is a Lazzie, so the sets Razzie and Lazzie are disjoint. Step 3: since Bloops sit entirely inside Razzies, and Razzies never overlap Lazzies, Bloops cannot overlap Lazzies either. So no — a Bloop cannot be a Lazzie."}
{"prompt": "A train leaves Station A at 60 mph. Two hours later, another train leaves the same station going 90 mph in the same direction. When will the second train catch up?", "response": "Let me think this through step by step. First, calculate how far the first train has traveled in its 2-hour head start: 60 mph × 2 hours = 120 miles ahead. Now, the second train is faster by 90 - 60 = 30 mph. This is the closing speed — the rate at which the gap shrinks. To close a 120-mile gap at 30 mph: time = 120 / 30 = 4 hours. So the second train catches up 4 hours after it departs, which is 6 hours after the first train left. Verification: train 1 traveled 60 × 6 = 360 miles; train 2 traveled 90 × 4 = 360 miles. They meet at 360 miles from Station A."}
{"prompt": "In a room, there are some people and some dogs. If you count all the heads, you get 12. If you count all the legs, you get 34. How many people and how many dogs are there?", "response": "Let me solve this systematically. Let P = number of people and D = number of dogs. Each person has 1 head and 2 legs. Each dog has 1 head and 4 legs. From the heads count: P + D = 12. From the legs count: 2P + 4D = 34. I can simplify the legs equation by dividing by 2: P + 2D = 17. Now subtract the heads equation from this: (P + 2D) - (P + D) = 17 - 12, which gives D = 5. So there are 5 dogs. Substituting back: P + 5 = 12, so P = 7. Check: 7 people + 5 dogs = 12 heads ✓; 7×2 + 5×4 = 14 + 20 = 34 legs ✓. Answer: 7 people and 5 dogs."}
{"prompt": "Consider the sequence: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, ... What is the 15th term?", "response": "This is the Fibonacci sequence, where each term is the sum of the two preceding terms. Let me compute carefully: F(1) = 1, F(2) = 1, F(3) = 1+1 = 2, F(4) = 1+2 = 3, F(5) = 2+3 = 5, F(6) = 3+5 = 8, F(7) = 5+8 = 13, F(8) = 8+13 = 21, F(9) = 13+21 = 34, F(10) = 21+34 = 55, F(11) = 34+55 = 89, F(12) = 55+89 = 144, F(13) = 89+144 = 233, F(14) = 144+233 = 377, F(15) = 233+377 = 610. The 15th term is 610."}
{"prompt": "You have a 3-gallon jug and a 5-gallon jug, and an unlimited supply of water. How do you measure out exactly 4 gallons?", "response": "This is a classic water jug problem. Let me work through a solution step by step: Step 1: Fill the 5-gallon jug completely (5-gal: 5, 3-gal: 0). Step 2: Pour from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug until the 3-gallon jug is full (5-gal: 2, 3-gal: 3). Step 3: Empty the 3-gallon jug (5-gal: 2, 3-gal: 0). Step 4: Pour the 2 gallons from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug (5-gal: 0, 3-gal: 2). Step 5: Fill the 5-gallon jug completely again (5-gal: 5, 3-gal: 2). Step 6: Pour from the 5-gallon jug into the 3-gallon jug until the 3-gallon jug is full. Since it already has 2 gallons, it only needs 1 more gallon (5-gal: 4, 3-gal: 3). The 5-gallon jug now contains exactly 4 gallons!"}
{"prompt": "A clock shows 3:15. What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand?", "response": "Let me calculate this carefully. First, the minute hand: at 15 minutes past the hour, it points at the 3, which is exactly 90 degrees from 12 o'clock (each minute mark = 6 degrees, so 15 * 6 = 90 degrees). Now for the hour hand: at 3:00 it would point exactly at the 3, but by 3:15 it has moved further. The hour hand moves 30 degrees per hour (360 degrees / 12 hours = 30 degrees) and 0.5 degrees per minute (30 degrees / 60 min = 0.5 degrees). At 3:15, the hour hand is at: 3 * 30 + 15 * 0.5 = 90 + 7.5 = 97.5 degrees from 12 o'clock. The angle between them is: 97.5 - 90 = 7.5 degrees. So the angle is 7.5 degrees."}
